question.stem:
The course excerpt explains why interleaved execution makes shared data structures unsafe.

A student proposes this lock routine for a kernel shared by multiple CPUs:

acquire():
  if (lock == 0) {
    lock = 1;
  }

Two CPUs run acquire() at the same time while lock is initially 0.

Using the excerpt, explain briefly why this check-then-set routine is not a safe lock.
